\newcommand{\NFCI}{\mathrm{NFCI}}
\newcommand{\ggdp}{\mathrm{ggdp}}
\newcommand{\ggdph}{\mathrm{ggdp\_h1}}
\newcommand{\bN}{\beta_{\NFCI}}
\newcommand{\bG}{\beta_{\ggdp}}
\title{Using QRfs: Fixed-Smoothing Inference\\for Growth Vulnerability}
\author{}
\date{\today}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

This vignette shows how to specify a model, state a hypothesis, and read the result using
\texttt{QRfs}. The application follows the predictive quantile regression of
\citet{adrian2019}. The tests use the fixed-smoothing
inference procedures of \citet{chen2026}.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}lX@{}}
\toprule
Function & Purpose \\
\midrule
\texttt{qrfstest\_stacked} & Tests a hypothesis jointly at a few specified quantiles. \\
\texttt{qrfstest\_sup} & Tests a hypothesis uniformly over a range of quantiles. \\
\texttt{qrfstest\_shape} & Tests monotonicity or homogeneity. \\
\texttt{qrhac} & Tests a hypothesis jointly at a few specified quantiles with the small-$b$ or
  large-$K$ chi-square critical value. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{center}

\section{Load the data and specify the model}

Start by loading the package and the data:

<<setup>>=
library(QRfs)
data(adrian)
head(adrian, 3)
@

\texttt{ggdp} is the annualized real GDP growth rate. The variable \texttt{ggdp} is the rate in
quarter $t$, and \texttt{ggdp\_h1} is the rate in quarter $t+1$.
The data contain 171 usable observations. The \texttt{quarter} column labels quarter $t$,
from 1973Q1 to 2015Q3.

\citet{adrian2019} use current real GDP growth to measure economic conditions
and the Chicago Fed National Financial Conditions Index (NFCI) to measure financial
conditions. A higher NFCI indicates tighter financial conditions. They model
``the full distribution of future real GDP growth as a function of current financial
and economic conditions'' \citep[p.~1264]{adrian2019}.

For the one-quarter horizon, $\ggdph_t=\ggdp_{t+1}$ and $x_t'=(1,\ggdp_t,\NFCI_t)$.
Using the notation of \citet[Section~2]{chen2026}, the conditional quantile model is
\begin{equation}
 Q_{\ggdph_t}(\tau\mid x_t)=x_t'\beta(\tau)
 =\beta_0(\tau)+\bG(\tau)\ggdp_t+\bN(\tau)\NFCI_t,
 \qquad \tau\in\mathcal T\subset(0,1).
 \label{eq:model}
\end{equation}
In R, model~\eqref{eq:model} is \texttt{ggdp\_h1 \textasciitilde\ ggdp + NFCI}.

The examples use ordinary QR estimates from \texttt{quantreg} and HAR covariance
estimators, which allow for heteroskedasticity and autocorrelation of the QR scores.
Choose \texttt{arg\_har\_method = "ker-har"} for the Bartlett kernel, or
\texttt{"os-har"} for the DCT-II orthonormal series. Fixed-smoothing inference keeps
$b=M/T$ or $K$ fixed in the asymptotic approximation \citep[Sections~3--4]{chen2026}.

The examples examine the predictive power of financial and economic conditions for
future GDP growth using tests of the quantile coefficients.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig_biv.pdf}
\caption{Estimated quantile coefficients in model~\eqref{eq:model}, on the grid
$0.05,0.10,\ldots,0.95$. Circles mark $0.1,0.5,0.9$. Shading marks the lower-tail range
$(0,0.25]$.}
\label{fig:slopes}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:slopes} shows the estimated quantile coefficients for financial
and economic conditions. The examples below test their significance, equality across
quantiles, and monotonicity.

\section{Joint restrictions at specified quantiles: stack-Wald tests}

\subsection{Stacked test with Bartlett HAR}

Does the dependence of future GDP growth on current financial conditions differ
across the lower tail, median, and upper tail, holding current GDP growth fixed?
The relevant hypothesis would be
\[
 H_0:\ \bN(0.1)=\bN(0.5)=\bN(0.9),
 \qquad H_A:\ \text{at least one equality fails}.
\]
The test statistic is the stack-Wald statistic of \citet[equation~16]{chen2026},
\[
 W=T\,r(\hat\beta(\bar\tau_m))'
   \big[R\,\hat D(\bar\tau_m)^{-1}\hat\Lambda(\bar\tau_m,\bar\tau_m)\hat D(\bar\tau_m)^{-1}R'\big]^{-1}
   r(\hat\beta(\bar\tau_m)),
\]
with $\bar\tau_m=\{0.1,0.5,0.9\}$, $r(\beta(\bar\tau_m))=R\beta(\bar\tau_m)$ the two equalities and
$q=\operatorname{rank}(R)=2$. Under the Bartlett kernel with $b=M/T$ fixed,
$W\Rightarrow W_q(1)'[P(\tilde W_q,b)]^{-1}W_q(1)$, the pivotal fixed-$b$ limit of
\citet[equation~18]{chen2026}, from which the critical value is simulated. Under the OS estimator
with $K$ fixed, $\frac{K-q+1}{qK}W\Rightarrow F_{q,K-q+1}$ \citep[equation~19]{chen2026}.
\texttt{arg\_seed = 1} fixes the simulation seed, so that
the printed numbers can be reproduced.

<<stacked_ker>>=
stacked_ker <- qrfstest_stacked(
  arg_formula = ggdp_h1 ~ ggdp + NFCI,
  arg_data = adrian,
  arg_hypothesis = "NFCI[0.1] = NFCI[0.5] = NFCI[0.9]",
  arg_har_method = "ker-har",
  arg_seed = 1
)
stacked_ker
@

The selected bandwidth is
\texttt{stacked\_ker\$M\_effective}, which equals 3.

\subsection{Stacked test with OS HAR}

Change \texttt{arg\_har\_method} to use the orthonormal-series estimator:

<<stacked_os>>=
stacked_os <- qrfstest_stacked(
  ggdp_h1 ~ ggdp + NFCI, adrian,
  "NFCI[0.1] = NFCI[0.5] = NFCI[0.9]", "os-har"
)
stacked_os
@

The same hypothesis can be supplied as two equality strings:

<<equivalent_stacked_inputs>>=
qrfstest_stacked(
  ggdp_h1 ~ ggdp + NFCI, adrian,
  arg_hypothesis = c("NFCI[0.1] = NFCI[0.5]",
                     "NFCI[0.5] = NFCI[0.9]"),
  arg_har_method = "os-har"
)
@

Equivalently, write the differences as equal to zero:

<<equivalent_stacked_differences>>=
qrfstest_stacked(
  ggdp_h1 ~ ggdp + NFCI, adrian,
  arg_hypothesis = c("NFCI[0.1] - NFCI[0.5] = 0",
                     "NFCI[0.5] - NFCI[0.9] = 0"),
  arg_har_method = "os-har"
)
@

To test equality across the three specified quantiles jointly for NFCI and GDP growth, use
\[
 H_0:\ \begin{cases}
 \bG(0.1)=\bG(0.5)=\bG(0.9),\\
 \bN(0.1)=\bN(0.5)=\bN(0.9).
 \end{cases}
\]
The statistic is $W$ above with $q=4$ restrictions, and the limits are those of
\citet[equations~18 and~19]{chen2026} with $q=4$.

<<joint>>=
joint <- qrfstest_stacked(
  ggdp_h1 ~ ggdp + NFCI, adrian,
  arg_hypothesis = c(
    "ggdp[0.1] = ggdp[0.5] = ggdp[0.9]",
    "NFCI[0.1] = NFCI[0.5] = NFCI[0.9]"
  ),
  arg_har_method = "os-har"
)
joint
@

\subsection{Stacked test with small-$b$ and large-$K$ chi-square critical values}

Under traditional asymptotics, $b\to0$ and $K\to\infty$ as $T\to\infty$, labeled small-$b$ and
large-$K$ asymptotics. Because small-$b$ and large-$K$ limits rely on consistency of the variance
estimator, they do not depend on the kernel, bandwidth, or $K$ \citep[Section~2]{chen2026}, and the
stack-Wald statistic has the $\chi^2_q$ limit. \texttt{qrhac} computes the same statistic as
\texttt{qrfstest\_stacked} with the $\chi^2_q$ critical value. The kernel HAC estimator was studied
by \citet{galvao2024} under traditional asymptotics for the bandwidth, with the MSE-optimal bandwidth
of their Corollary~5.1. The test-based rules of \citet[Section~4.5]{chen2026} are based on
higher-order expansions of the fixed-smoothing limits. The rule for the statistic in \texttt{qrhac} is
therefore \texttt{arg\_M = "mse\_optimal\_ar"} or \texttt{arg\_K = "mse\_optimal\_var"}.
\texttt{"test\_optimal"} or a number may be given instead, to use the same $M$ or $K$ as
\texttt{qrfstest\_stacked} \citep[Section~5]{chen2026}.
Nothing is simulated, so there is no \texttt{arg\_seed}.

Does the dependence of future GDP growth on current financial conditions differ across the
$0.05$, $0.5$ and $0.95$ quantiles?
\[
 H_0:\ \bN(0.05)=\bN(0.5)=\bN(0.95),
 \qquad H_A:\ \text{at least one equality fails}.
\]
The statistic is the stack-Wald statistic $W$ with $\bar\tau_m=\{0.05,0.5,0.95\}$ and $q=2$. Under
small-$b$ or large-$K$ asymptotics $W\Rightarrow\chi^2_q$, the limit of \citet[equation~18]{chen2026}
as $b\to0$ and of \citet[equation~19]{chen2026} as $K\to\infty$, so the critical value is the
$(1-\alpha)$ quantile of $\chi^2_2$.

<<hac_ker>>=
hac_ker <- qrhac(
  ggdp_h1 ~ ggdp + NFCI, adrian,
  "NFCI[0.05] = NFCI[0.5] = NFCI[0.95]", "ker-har"
)
hac_ker
@

<<hac_os>>=
hac_os <- qrhac(
  ggdp_h1 ~ ggdp + NFCI, adrian,
  "NFCI[0.05] = NFCI[0.5] = NFCI[0.95]", "os-har"
)
hac_os
@

At a single quantile, the null is $H_0:\bN(0.9)=0$, with $q=1$ and the $\chi^2_1$ limit.

<<hac_single>>=
hac_single <- qrhac(
  ggdp_h1 ~ ggdp + NFCI, adrian,
  "NFCI[0.9] = 0", "ker-har"
)
hac_single
@

The result is printed with the method in its first line, small-$b$ Bartlett kernel HAC or
large-$K$ orthonormal series HAR. \texttt{summary()} and \texttt{coef()} apply as for
\texttt{qrfstest\_stacked}.

\section{Uniform test}

\subsection{Uniform sup test}

This example uses the uniform sup test to examine the predictive power of the financial
condition for future GDP growth.
The hypothesis for the NFCI coefficient over $\mathcal T=(0,1)$ is
\[
 H_0:\ \bN(\tau)=0\ \text{for all }\tau\in(0,1),
 \qquad H_A:\ \bN(\tau)\ne0\ \text{for some }\tau\in(0,1).
\]
The test statistic is the supremum over $\mathcal T$ of the Wald statistic at each quantile,
evaluated on the grid $\{\tau_1,\dots,\tau_n\}$ that discretizes $\mathcal T$:
\[
 W_{\sup}(\mathcal T)=\max_{1\le i\le n}W(\tau_i),\qquad
 W(\tau)=T\,r(\hat\beta(\tau))'\big[R(\hat\beta(\tau))\hat\Sigma(\tau)R(\hat\beta(\tau))'\big]^{-1}
 r(\hat\beta(\tau)),
\]
the latter being the Wald statistic of \citet[equation~2]{chen2026} with
$\hat\Sigma(\tau)=\hat D(\tau)^{-1}\hat\Lambda(\tau)\hat D(\tau)^{-1}$, as in Section~2.1 at a single
quantile. Here $r(\beta(\tau))=\bN(\tau)$, and in the limit below $R_0=R(\beta_0(\tau))$.
Its asymptotic distribution follows from \citet[equation~9]{chen2026}:
\[
 W_{\sup}(\mathcal T)\Rightarrow\max_{1\le i\le n}
 \big(R_0D(\tau_i)^{-1}G(1,\tau_i)\big)'[H^{\iota}(\tau_i)]^{-1}\big(R_0D(\tau_i)^{-1}G(1,\tau_i)\big),
 \qquad \iota=\mathrm{KE},\mathrm{OS},
\]
where, for the Bartlett kernel and the OS estimator respectively,
\[
 H^{\mathrm{KE}}(\tau)=R_0D(\tau)^{-1}P(\tilde G(r,\tau),b)D(\tau)^{-1}R_0',\qquad
 H^{\mathrm{OS}}(\tau)=R_0D(\tau)^{-1}\Big[\tfrac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^K G_k(1,\tau)G_k(1,\tau)'\Big]D(\tau)^{-1}R_0'.
\]
This limit depends on $\Lambda(\tau_1,\tau_2)$ and is not pivotal. The critical value is its
$(1-\alpha)$ quantile, simulated by Algorithm~1 of \citet{chen2026}.

<<sup_nfci>>=
sup_nfci <- qrfstest_sup(
  ggdp_h1 ~ ggdp + NFCI, adrian,
  "NFCI = 0", "ker-har",
  arg_seed = 1
)
sup_nfci
@

When \texttt{arg\_tau\_grid} is not specified (\texttt{NULL}), the default grid is
$\{0.1,0.3,0.5,0.7,0.9\}$ and the printed range is $(0,1)$. The grid discretizes the
range. The calculation does not fit the continuum of quantiles.

To examine the predictive power of the financial condition at the lower tails,
apply the same test over $\mathcal T=(0,0.25]$. The null is
\[
 H_0:\ \bN(\tau)=0\quad\text{for all }\tau\in(0,0.25].
\]
The statistic and its limit are those above, on a grid over $(0,0.25]$.

<<sup_nfci_lower>>=
sup_nfci_lower <- qrfstest_sup(
  ggdp_h1 ~ ggdp + NFCI, adrian,
  "NFCI = 0", "ker-har",
  arg_tau_grid = list(range = c(0, 0.25), n = 5),
  arg_seed = 1
)
sup_nfci_lower
@

The same grid can be supplied directly:

<<sup_nfci_lower_grid>>=
sup_nfci_lower_grid <- qrfstest_sup(
  ggdp_h1 ~ ggdp + NFCI, adrian,
  "NFCI = 0", "ker-har",
  arg_tau_grid = c(0.05, 0.10, 0.15, 0.20, 0.25),
  arg_seed = 1
)
@

These two calls give the same statistic, critical value, and p-value.
Changing the range changes the hypothesis.
Changing the grid changes its numerical evaluation.

For a joint test of the predictive power of the financial and economic conditions, supply
\texttt{c("ggdp = 0", "NFCI = 0")} instead. This is a different null, with two
restrictions per quantile.

\subsection{Read and plot the uniform confidence band}

For a single restriction, the library computes a uniform confidence band following
\citet[Section~4.3]{chen2026}.

<<band, fig=TRUE, include=FALSE>>=
sup_nfci_lower <- qrfstest_sup(
  ggdp_h1 ~ ggdp + NFCI, adrian,
  "NFCI = 0", "ker-har",
  arg_tau_grid = list(range = c(0, 0.25), n = 5),
  arg_seed = 1
)
band <- uniform_CI_band(sup_nfci_lower)
# plot(sup_nfci_lower) draws the same band, as does arg_band_plot = TRUE in qrfstest_sup().
@

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.86\linewidth]{fig_band.pdf}
\caption{The 95\% uniform confidence band for the NFCI coefficient over the lower-tail grid.}
\end{figure}

\texttt{uniform\_CI\_band()} draws the band and returns its table invisibly, the same table as
\texttt{sup\_nfci\_lower\$band}, which contains the estimates and limits.
\texttt{sup\_nfci\_lower\$wald\_tau} contains the component statistics. With multiple
restrictions, \texttt{qrfstest\_sup()} returns the joint Wald statistics at each quantile in
\texttt{wald\_tau} and no band, and \texttt{uniform\_CI\_band()} stops.

\FloatBarrier

Similarly, to test whether GDP growth has predictive power for $\tau\in\mathcal T$,
the null is
\[
 H_0:\ \bG(\tau)=0\quad\text{for all }\tau\in\mathcal T.
\]
The statistic and its limit are those of the sup test above with $r(\beta(\tau))=\bG(\tau)$.
Use the following call:

<<sup_gdp>>=
sup_gdp <- qrfstest_sup(
  ggdp_h1 ~ ggdp + NFCI, adrian,
  "ggdp = 0", "ker-har",
  arg_seed = 1
)
sup_gdp
@

\section{Shape restrictions on the NFCI coefficient}

\subsection{Homogeneity test}

This example uses the homogeneity test to examine whether the quantile coefficient of NFCI is
constant across a range of quantiles. The homogeneity null in \citet[Section~4.4]{chen2026} is
\[
 H_0:\ \bN(\tau_2)=\bN(\tau_1)
       \quad\text{for all }\tau_1\ne\tau_2\in\mathcal T.
\]
On the grid $\{\tau_1,\dots,\tau_n\}$ the statistic is
\[
 t_H(\mathcal T)=\max_{2\le i\le n}\sqrt T\,\big|\hat\beta_{\NFCI}(\tau_i)-\hat\beta_{\NFCI}(\tau_{i-1})\big|
 \big/\hat\sigma(\tau_i,\tau_{i-1}),
\]
with $\hat\sigma(\tau_i,\tau_l)$ the estimated standard deviation of $\sqrt T$ times the difference
\citep[Section~4.4]{chen2026}. Under the null,
$t_H(\mathcal T)\Rightarrow\max_{2\le i\le n}|a_{i,i-1;j}'D(\bar\tau_n)^{-1}G(1,\bar\tau_n)|
\big/\sqrt{a_{i,i-1;j}'H^{\iota}(\bar\tau_n)a_{i,i-1;j}}$ \citep[Proposition~4.2]{chen2026}, where
$a_{i,l;j}$ selects $\beta_j(\tau_i)-\beta_j(\tau_l)$ from $\beta(\bar\tau_n)$, and
$H^{\iota}(\bar\tau_n)$ is the covariance functional of the limit: the kernel functional
$H(\bar\tau_n,b)$ of \citet[Proposition~4.2]{chen2026} for $\iota=\mathrm{KE}$, and its fixed-$K$
counterpart for $\iota=\mathrm{OS}$. The critical value is the $(1-\alpha)$ quantile of this
limit, simulated by Algorithm~3 of \citet{chen2026}.
Use \texttt{qrfstest\_shape()} with \texttt{arg\_test = "homogeneity"}:

<<homogeneous>>=
homogeneous <- qrfstest_shape(
  ggdp_h1 ~ ggdp + NFCI, adrian, "NFCI", "ker-har",
  arg_test = "homogeneity", arg_seed = 1
)
homogeneous
@

To supply a different grid, use the same grid argument as for the sup test:

<<homogeneous_grid>>=
homogeneous_grid <- qrfstest_shape(
  ggdp_h1 ~ ggdp + NFCI, adrian, "NFCI", "ker-har",
  arg_test = "homogeneity",
  arg_tau_grid = c(0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 0.9),
  arg_seed = 1
)
homogeneous_grid$p_value
@

Choose the range and grid to reflect the empirical question.

\subsection{Monotonicity test}

This example tests whether the quantile coefficient of NFCI is weakly increasing across quantiles.
The monotonicity null in \citet[Section~4.4]{chen2026} is
\[
 H_0:\ \bN(\tau_2)-\bN(\tau_1)\ge0
       \quad\text{for all }\tau_2>\tau_1\text{ in }\mathcal T.
\]
The statistic is
\[
 t_M(\mathcal T)=\min_{i>l}\sqrt T\,\big\{\hat\beta_{\NFCI}(\tau_i)-\hat\beta_{\NFCI}(\tau_l)\big\}
 \big/\hat\sigma(\tau_i,\tau_l)
\]
over the adjacent pairs $l=i-1$ (\texttt{"monotonicity\_adjacent"}) or over all pairs
(\texttt{"monotonicity\_allpair"}), reported with its sign reversed so that large values reject.
Under the least favorable null of a constant coefficient,
$t_M(\mathcal T)\Rightarrow\min_{i>l}a_{i,l;j}'D(\bar\tau_n)^{-1}G(1,\bar\tau_n)
\big/\sqrt{a_{i,l;j}'H^{\iota}(\bar\tau_n)a_{i,l;j}}$ \citep[Proposition~4.2]{chen2026}, with $a_{i,l;j}$
and $H^{\iota}(\bar\tau_n)$ as above. The reported critical value is the $(1-\alpha)$ quantile of
$-t_M(\mathcal T)$, simulated by
Algorithm~3 of \citet{chen2026}.
Use \texttt{arg\_alternative = "decreasing"} to test this null.

<<monotone_adjacent>>=
monotone_adjacent <- qrfstest_shape(
  ggdp_h1 ~ ggdp + NFCI, adrian, "NFCI", "ker-har",
  arg_test = "monotonicity_adjacent",
  arg_alternative = "decreasing", arg_seed = 1
)
monotone_adjacent
@

The adjacent procedure compares consecutive fitted quantiles, as in
\citet[Section~5]{chen2026}.

For all pairwise comparisons, as in \citet[Section~4.4]{chen2026}, change the test choice:

<<monotone_all>>=
monotone_all <- qrfstest_shape(
  ggdp_h1 ~ ggdp + NFCI, adrian, "NFCI", "ker-har",
  arg_test = "monotonicity_allpair",
  arg_alternative = "decreasing", arg_seed = 1
)
monotone_all
@

Both procedures test the weakly increasing null on the chosen grid, but use different
sets of comparisons and can give different statistics and critical values.
Negative reported statistics are allowed.

To reverse the null and alternative, set \texttt{arg\_alternative = "increasing"}.
To choose the grid explicitly, add \texttt{arg\_tau\_grid}, for example:

<<monotone_grid>>=
monotone_grid <- qrfstest_shape(
  ggdp_h1 ~ ggdp + NFCI, adrian, "NFCI", "ker-har",
  arg_test = "monotonicity_adjacent",
  arg_alternative = "decreasing",
  arg_tau_grid = c(0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 0.9),
  arg_seed = 1
)
@

\Needspace{12\baselineskip}
\section{Options related to computing smoothing parameter \texorpdfstring{$b$ or $K$}{b or K}}

The same hypothesis can be evaluated with either HAR method and with automatic or
user-provided smoothing. These choices generally change the statistic and critical value.
By default, the test statistic uses \texttt{arg\_M = "test\_optimal"} for Bartlett HAR
and \texttt{arg\_K = "test\_optimal"} for OS HAR with the DCT-II basis.
\texttt{qrhac} uses \texttt{arg\_M = "mse\_optimal\_ar"} and \texttt{arg\_K = "mse\_optimal\_var"} by default.
For sup and shape tests, the nuisance covariance uses
\texttt{arg\_M\_lambda = "mse\_optimal\_ar"} for Bartlett HAR and
\texttt{arg\_K\_lambda = "mse\_optimal\_var"} for OS HAR.

The test-based rule follows \citet[Section~4.5]{chen2026}. Sup and shape tests use
the stacked rule with the restrictions for their test. Automatic $K$ selection uses a
minimum of the test dimension plus four: $q+4$ for stacked, $q_0+4$ for sup, and 5 for
shape. Nuisance selection uses $mp+4$. \texttt{qrhac} uses the minimum $q$, since its $\chi^2_q$ limit
requires only that the variance estimator be full rank. The data-dependent rules also include
\texttt{"simple"}, \texttt{"mse\_optimal\_ar"}, and \texttt{"mse\_optimal\_var"}.

The test-based and VAR-based MSE rules use a VAR(1) approximation to the stacked scores.
The AR-based MSE rule uses a separate AR(1) for each score column.
By default (\texttt{arg\_var\_fit = "gaussian"}), a bivariate-normal approximation for
indicator dependence is combined with regressor dependence, following \citet{galvao2024}
and \citet[Section~4.5]{chen2026}.
The default threshold is $\Phi^{-1}(\tau)$.
Setting \texttt{arg\_threshold\_method = "empirical"} uses $\hat q_\tau$, the empirical
quantile of standardized residuals.
With \texttt{arg\_var\_fit = "ols"}, the AR(1) or VAR(1) is fitted directly to the scores
by least squares. The requirement $T-1>mp$ applies only to the OLS VAR fit.
In either case the fitted coefficients are held within the stationarity bound
\texttt{arg\_var\_stationary} ($0.97$ by default). Under \texttt{"unrestricted"} a fit that is not
stationary is an error.

For example, evaluate the stacked equality test at a user-provided $M=12$ or $K=20$:

<<fixed_smoothing>>=
fixed_ker <- qrfstest_stacked(
  ggdp_h1 ~ ggdp + NFCI, adrian,
  "NFCI[0.1] = NFCI[0.5] = NFCI[0.9]", "ker-har",
  arg_M = 12, arg_seed = 1
)
fixed_os <- qrfstest_stacked(
  ggdp_h1 ~ ggdp + NFCI, adrian,
  "NFCI[0.1] = NFCI[0.5] = NFCI[0.9]", "os-har",
  arg_K = 20
)
@

For sup or shape tests, test smoothing and nuisance smoothing are separate inputs:

<<nuisance_choice>>=
sup_os <- qrfstest_sup(
  ggdp_h1 ~ ggdp + NFCI, adrian, "NFCI = 0", "os-har",
  arg_tau_grid = list(range = c(0, 0.25), n = 5),
  arg_K = 40, arg_K_lambda = 30, arg_seed = 1
)
@

Numeric choices are used as supplied within the admissible limits. For this data,
$1\le M\le171$ and $K\le170$. Test $K$ must also cover the rank of each Wald statistic.
The nuisance choice is used to estimate the long-run covariance for critical-value simulation.
Stacked tests do not require it.

When simulation is used, the functions generate 10\,000 draws. Bartlett simulations use
1\,000 Brownian bridge grid intervals. The p-value is the fraction of simulated statistics
at least as large as the observed one. When none of the draws reaches the observed
statistic, the p-value is printed as \texttt{p-val < 0.0001}, indicating the simulation
resolution rather than an upper confidence bound on the limiting p-value.

The calls above pass \texttt{arg\_seed = 1} to reproduce their simulated critical values and
p-values. Calling \texttt{set.seed()} immediately before a test is equivalent:

<<external_seed>>=
set.seed(1)
sup_external_seed <- qrfstest_sup(
  ggdp_h1 ~ ggdp + NFCI, adrian, "NFCI = 0", "ker-har"
)
@

This reproduces \texttt{sup\_nfci}. Without a seed (\texttt{arg\_seed = NULL}, the default) a test
uses and advances the current random-number stream. Use the same seed when comparing equivalent
inputs.
Set \texttt{arg\_alpha} to change the significance level. It is also used by the
\texttt{"test\_optimal"} rule.

The default Hessian estimator is \texttt{arg\_hinv\_method = "ker"}, the kernel estimator of
eq.~(4) of the paper as implemented in \texttt{quantreg}. The \texttt{"nid"} option selects its sparsity
estimator instead. A different Hessian choice changes the statistics, not the procedures.

The quantile regressions are fitted by \texttt{quantreg::rq}. Its solver is chosen by
\texttt{arg\_rq\_method}: \texttt{"br"} (default) is the Barrodale--Roberts simplex, \texttt{"fn"}
the Frisch--Newton interior point used in the code of \citet{adrian2019}. On these data the
two agree to $10^{-7}$ in every coefficient.

\Needspace{20\baselineskip}
\section{Use the returned results}

Printing a result shows the hypothesis, statistic, critical value, p-value, and selected
smoothing. The object also stores these values for subsequent analysis:

<<result_fields>>=
data.frame(
  statistic = stacked_ker$stat,
  critical_value = unname(stacked_ker$cv),
  p_value = stacked_ker$p_value,
  reject = stacked_ker$reject,
  M = stacked_ker$M_effective
)
round(coef(stacked_os), 3)
@

\texttt{summary()} adds what the printout leaves out: the estimates, the limit that calibrates
the critical value, and the nuisance smoothing of a sup or shape test.

<<summary>>=
summary(sup_nfci_lower)
@

Use \texttt{K\_effective} for selected OS smoothing, and \texttt{M\_lambda} or
\texttt{K\_lambda} for nuisance smoothing. Shape results also contain
\texttt{difference} and \texttt{t\_ratio}. Sup results contain \texttt{wald\_tau}
and, for one restriction, \texttt{band}. The reference manual describes all arguments
and return fields.

